<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Nayagarh State</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Nayagarh</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A princely state in the hills south-west of Cuttack in Odisha, ruled by a family of Rajput descent until 1948.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tradition holds that the ruling family descended from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghel">Baghel</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> who came from Rewa and founded the state around 1500. Its early history is not well recorded.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Nayagarh was an agricultural state of valleys and wooded hills. It was tributary to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> and, from 1803, one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orissa-tributary-states">Orissa Tributary Mahals</a> under British supervision. Little detailed history survives.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Nayagarh merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a> on 1 January 1948 and is now the headquarters of Nayagarh district.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/nayagarh-state.jpg" alt="Nayagarh State" width="320" height="252"><figcaption>Image: Great Britain India Office · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Daspalla-Nayagarh-Imperial_Gazetteer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nayagarh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Odia</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nayagarh</dd></dl></aside></div>
